// Tracked wrapper for the liters (page-replication) linkage, so a clean checkout that has never run
// Rust/build-ios.sh still generates and builds. Included from Config/BundleId.xcconfig, which
// project.yml applies project-wide, so every target inherits these.
//
// Liters.xcframework is a BUILD ARTIFACT, not a tracked file: the device slice alone is ~83 MB, and
// three slices plus a macOS slice is far past what belongs in git. It is produced by
// `Rust/build-ios.sh`, which ALSO writes the untracked Config/LitersLocal.xcconfig included at the
// bottom of this file. That is deliberate — the search paths below name specific xcframework slice
// directories, and having the build script emit them from the layout `xcodebuild -create-xcframework`
// actually produced is the only way they cannot drift from it.
//
// The three variables default to EMPTY here. project.yml appends them unconditionally
// (LIBRARY_SEARCH_PATHS / SWIFT_INCLUDE_PATHS / OTHER_LDFLAGS), so with no local file the appends
// expand to nothing: no -l flag, no search path, and a default build links exactly what it links
// today. Absence of the xcframework is therefore not a build error for anyone who does not want it.
//
// LITERS_LDFLAGS is what actually pulls the archive in. It is kept separate from the search path so
// that "the paths are configured" and "the library is linked" stay two decisions: a build with the
// paths set but LITERS_LDFLAGS empty compiles the generated bindings' module import and fails at
// link, which is a clearer error than a missing module.
LITERS_LIB_DIR =
LITERS_HEADER_DIR =
LITERS_LDFLAGS =

// Set by build-ios.sh alongside the paths above. Gates every line of Swift that touches liters
// (Strand/CloudSync/Generated/, and the round-trip tests), so a checkout without the xcframework
// compiles none of it rather than failing to resolve `import liters_ffiFFI`:
//
//   OTHER_SWIFT_FLAGS = $(inherited) -D LITERS
//
// NOT SWIFT_ACTIVE_COMPILATION_CONDITIONS, even though that is the idiomatic setting and what
// OuraSecrets.xcconfig uses for OURA_CLOUD_IMPORT / CLOUD_SYNC. Those work because OuraConfig.xcconfig
// is a TARGET-level xcconfig, which outranks the project build settings; this file is included from
// the PROJECT-level xcconfig, which xcodegen's own `SWIFT_ACTIVE_COMPILATION_CONDITIONS = DEBUG`
// (written into the project's Debug config, with no $(inherited)) sits above and silently overwrites.
// Verified with `xcodebuild -showBuildSettings`: the LITERS entry vanishes, DEBUG survives.
#include? "LitersLocal.xcconfig"
